  "processing_mode": null,
  "analysis": {
    "why_you_should_care": "The interesting part is not the rewrite itself. It is how the project preserves ABI compatibility while changing the implementation language.",
    "what_happened": "A team reimplemented a widely linked C library in Rust and shipped it as a drop-in replacement. Existing binaries keep calling the same symbols.",
    "how_it_works": "C API \u2192 ABI compatibility \u2192 Rust implementation \u2192 FFI boundary \u2192 ownership translation \u2192 platform shims \u2192 behavioral tests \u2192 published benchmarks from the authors only.",
    "under_the_hood": "Public headers stay C. The shared object exports the original symbol names. Internally, Rust owns buffers and converts them at the FFI edge. Platform-specific POSIX calls remain behind a thin C-compatible veneer so callers do not need to know the implementation language.",
    "why_they_built_it": "Memory safety in a library that sits under other people's processes, without asking those people to recompile the universe.",
    "what_was_difficult": "Matching undefined-but-relied-upon C behavior, errno conventions, pointer lifetimes that outlive a Rust frame, and build systems that assume cc.",
    "performance": "The available sources do not establish independent benchmark numbers. Use only figures published by the project and treat them as author-reported.",
    "trade_offs": "You gain a memory-safe core and a chance at safer internals. You pay in FFI friction, a more complex build, and the obligation to mimic C's worst habits.",
    "what_is_surprising": "The hard part is not writing Rust. The hard part is pretending you are still C.",
    "quick_read": "A Rust rewrite of a C library that keeps the old ABI so nothing else has to change \u2014 except the insides.",
